{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"fb779ab54865f154f06158ad621d4f338318644cd5e62cd5a19f4d2b61e3de06","slug":"pages.m11-random-variables-1-of-2","location":"/pages/128-m11-random-variables-1-of-2.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Random Variables: Numbers from Random Experiments","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"abbreviations":{"ANOVA":"Analysis of Variance","CI":"Confidence Interval","CLT":"Central Limit Theorem","EDA":"Exploratory Data Analysis","IQR":"Interquartile Range","SD":"Standard Deviation","SE":"Standard Error","SRS":"Simple Random Sample","INCREASE":null,"INCREASED":null,"DECREASES":null,"OBSERVED":null,"ROSE":null},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"128-m11-random-variables-1-of-2.md","url":"/textbook/build/128-m11-random-varia-02ee1ca4d086351203e3dc523183b695.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the previous two modules we’ve learned principles and tools that help us find probabilities of events in general. Now that we’ve become proficient at doing that, we’ll talk about random variables. Just like any other variable, random variables can take on multiple values. What differentiates random variables from other variables is that the values for these variables are determined by a random trial, random sample, or simulation. The probabilities for the values can be determined by theoretical or observational means. Such probabilities play a vital role in the theory behind statistical inference, our ultimate goal in this course.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"yC411htcRN"}],"key":"KPDd2hiVNz"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introduction","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"tz5T7f2PMT"}],"identifier":"introduction","label":"Introduction","html_id":"introduction","implicit":true,"key":"rdGzTKFnn6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We first discussed variables in the Exploratory Data Analysis portion of the course. A variable is a characteristic of an individual. We also made an important distinction between ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"p2vTQaFSQS"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"categorical variable","identifier":"term-categorical variable","children":[{"type":"text","value":"categorical variables","key":"kn1JarymzR"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-categorical-variable","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"cJdg0VDfV1"},{"type":"text","value":", whose values are groups or categories (and an individual can be placed into one of them), and ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"U1e2pW2CjS"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"quantitative variable","identifier":"term-quantitative variable","children":[{"type":"text","value":"quantitative variables","key":"f1CMeLT5hv"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-quantitative-variable","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"cWdEPhgpVB"},{"type":"text","value":", which have numerical values for which arithmetic operations make sense. In the previous two modules, we focused mostly on events which arise when there is a categorical variable in the background: blood type, pierced ears (yes/no), gender, on-time delivery (yes/no), side effect (yes/no), etc. Now we will begin to consider quantitative variables that arise when a random experiment is performed. We will need to define this new type of variable.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"xX0hcNyyE1"}],"key":"tvRUZnWOD0"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition: Random Variable","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"f5wL1x7YtO"}],"key":"SbDTTcAp3V"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A ","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"Af6aGN9bQJ"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"random variable","identifier":"term-random variable","children":[{"type":"text","value":"random variable","key":"tWqC6z2VHf"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-random-variable","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"nV5z9ZdkY8"},{"type":"text","value":" assigns a unique numerical value to the outcome of a random experiment.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"AhvTnPi97I"}],"key":"G5v1yRYQNO"}],"class":"note","key":"U6hMCM4vJs"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A random variable can be thought of as a function that associates exactly one of the possible numerical outcomes to each trial of a random experiment. However, that number can be the same for many of the trials.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"TdOhqhk8d8"}],"key":"dw7wqcuwLd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before we go any further, here are some simple examples:","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"YRkwsT4hRG"}],"key":"xSgKWKjB6L"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Theoretical","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"tJzKXvwiWP"}],"key":"fhzoDlpq82"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider the random experiment of flipping a coin twice. The sample space of possible outcomes is S = { HH, HT, TH, TT }.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"GmlMWwZiw3"}],"key":"z3AOm9spMA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now, let’s ","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"YtigX3xqCe"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"define the variable X to be the number of tails","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"NcI7P5FpgE"}],"key":"Z8sU3chJUR"},{"type":"text","value":" that the random experiment will produce.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"PSzpaGPDjh"}],"key":"XrkbgpkWQy"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the outcome is HH, we have no tails, so the value for X is 0.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"xKvg9VeNLn"}],"key":"FCocxivNv6"}],"key":"vnRVmDyI8z"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the outcome is HT, we got one tail, so the value for X is 1.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"kOWuSVsCSF"}],"key":"rT9drr2jGO"}],"key":"aeamWfYApG"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the outcome is TH, we again got one tail, so the value for X is 1.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"W4jWO6e5ds"}],"key":"Y5Bboiardx"}],"key":"SLz1xUkeeX"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lastly, if the outcome is TT, we got two tails, so the value for X is 2.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"bdgnB3gyzo"}],"key":"RMxxLpXSlF"}],"key":"ojCITsJa3x"}],"key":"dZuAYPEUUw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As the definition suggests, X is a quantitative variable that takes the possible values of 0, 1, or 2.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"gw6DSY7aBe"}],"key":"ik8hSyhdqH"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is random because we do not know which of the three values the variable will eventually take. We can ask questions like:","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"uoKoa2zBcN"}],"key":"rEIDTovqpf"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is the probability that X will be 2? In other words, what is the probability of getting 2 tails?","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"ewgWBQJB5v"}],"key":"CDy8pZW5Fd"}],"key":"wqMczAhGg6"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is the probability that X will be at least 1? In other words, what is the probability of getting at least 1 tail?","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"VbPjZDD9eE"}],"key":"WOfKSxMcge"}],"key":"zwY51VL4Mp"}],"key":"ADn2FYFBov"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As you can see, random variables are not really a new thing, but just a different way to look at the same problem.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"EEw4HhaV4f"}],"key":"fX8SVLoyDW"}],"class":"tip","key":"cw3qUMozsA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that if we had tossed a coin three times, the possible values for the number of tails would be 0, 1, 2, or 3. In general, if we toss a coin “n” times, the possible number of tails would be 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., or n.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"kw5LTfiX5o"}],"key":"NF2jSSVbeD"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Observational","position":{"start":{"line":43,"co